of the need to match different formats (with country code, area code, or phone number only) while ensuring that the data follows a standard format. The use of optional segments (`\\\\+?`, `\\\\(\\\\d{1,3}\\\\)\\\\s*\\\\d{0,4}`, and `[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}`) makes it more complex.\\n\\nFinal Output:\\n\\nHERE IS THE FINAL RESULT\\n\\nColumn Name: phone_number\\nColumn Type: object\\nBusiness Rules:\\na. The phone number should start with either '+' or '(' followed by a digit.\\nb. After the opening parenthesis, the first three digits represent the country code.\\nc. The last four digits are the phone number itself.\\nRegEx Pattern: r'^\\\\+?|\\\\(\\\\d{1,3}\\\\)\\\\s*\\\\d{0,4}|[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$'\\nComplexity: High\\nReasoning: The complexity is high because of the need to match different formats while ensuring that the data follows a standard format.", "total_unique": 1300}	2026-05-26 06:51:20.077576+00	a. The phone number should start with either '+' or '(' followed by a digit.\nb. After the opening parenthesis, the first three digits represent the country code.\nc. The last four digits are the phone number itself.	^\\+?|\\(\\d{1,3}\\)\\s*\\d{0,4}|[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$	llama3.2:3b	New Version	\N
